      <Graphics centerX="350" centerY="400" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="PMN, nilo-poyanco, SRI, caspi">Daidzein is an isoflavone present in a number of plants and herbs, including the Thai Kwao Krua (|FRAME: ORG-6203|), the Japanese Kudzu (|FRAME: TAX-3893|), and soybean. It is found in all soy products like tofu and textured vegetable protein. While the role of daidzein in the plant is probably that of an antioxidant, daidzein and related compounds also interact with animal and human estrogen receptors, acting as estrogen substitutes (giving them the name phytoestrogens).</Comment>
